/// What a duplicate in a `Set.of` or a `Map.of` literal is refused with,
/// family by family, exactly.
///
/// `Value.refuseDuplicate` is the fifth and last of the layout-directed
/// operations
/// [ADR 0064](../../../../docs/adr/0064-an-intrinsic-names-a-machine-not-a-method.md)'s
/// Decision 3 names, and the one that stays an intrinsic: see
/// `crates/cove-ir/src/lower/synth.rs`'s header for why. What it does that
/// is layout-directed is *render the key it names*, and that rendering ends
/// up inside a `RuntimeError` with a `rule` and a `help` beside it, which is
/// three sentences where `Inst::Trap` carries one. So this file is not a
/// before-and-after for a migration. It is the thing a migration would have
/// to keep, written down before anyone attempts one again.
///
/// # Two implementations, and that is the point of running it twice
///
/// The refusal is worded in two places that share no code:
/// `cove_runtime::builtins`' `duplicate_key_error`, over `MapKey`'s
/// `Display`, is what the tree-walking oracle raises, and
/// `cove_runtime::vm::intrinsics::key`'s `duplicate`, over `render_value`,
/// is what the linear-memory backend raises. Nothing but a corpus holds the
/// two to the same bytes, so this case carries a `both_backends` file and
/// every row below is asserted on each of them.
///
/// # The rows are the oracle, and they were written before anything ran
///
/// Every refusal quoted in a doc comment below was derived from the two Rust
/// sites named above and from the *specification* of the rendering that
/// `tests/e2e/values_value_render_into` pins — a `String` as its bytes with
/// nothing quoted and nothing escaped, an `Error` as its message alone, a
/// struct as `Name(field: value, ...)` in declaration order, an enum as
/// `Case` or `Case(part, ...)`, a run in `[]`, a set and a map in `{}`, a
/// `Duration` in the largest unit that divides it exactly, a range as the
/// operator it was written with, a box looked through — and not from what
/// either implementation printed. Each is:
///
/// ```text
/// `{method}` was given the {role} `{key}` more than once
///   rule: A literal with two identical keys is a mistake, not an intent; ...
///   help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different {role}
/// ```
///
/// with `{method}` and `{role}` the two `String` literals `std.set.of` and
/// `std.map.of` pass — `Set.of`/`element` and `Map.of`/`key` — and `{key}`
/// the rendering of the member found again. The `rule` is one constant
/// sentence and never varies, which is why the doc comments quote the
/// message and the `help` alone.
///
/// # Why a suite of `test fn`s and not a directory of `fail_` cases
///
/// A refusal ends the run that meets it, so a program can produce exactly
/// one — which is why `fail_key_duplicate_map` and `fail_key_duplicate_set`
/// are two directories for two rows. `cove test` catches each test's `Err`
/// and goes on to the next, so twenty-three refusals fit in one file and are
/// read against each other rather than across directories. The six passing
/// rows at the top are here for the same reason: what a literal with *no*
/// duplicate does belongs beside what one with a duplicate does.
///
/// # What is not here
///
/// A `Float`, a `Vector`, and anything holding one never reach this refusal
/// at all: `std.set.of` and `std.map.of` ask `core.admitKey` of every
/// element before they look for a duplicate, so the admission refuses first
/// and with its own words. `theAdmissionRefusesAFloatBeforeADuplicateIsLookedFor`
/// is the row that pins that ordering, and it is the one row below whose
/// refusal is not this operation's.
///
/// A key that nests deeper than `theDeepestNestingTwoKeysStillCompareAt`
/// goes is not here either, and the reason is measured: past 42 links the
/// *comparison* refuses — the backend's key walk is bounded at 128 steps and
/// the oracle's is not — so the two evaluators stop disagreeing about the
/// duplicate and start disagreeing about whether there is one. That is a
/// pre-existing difference between the evaluators rather than anything this
/// operation does, and a row that exercised it would be pinning the
/// disagreement.
/// A two-field struct, to see a composite key named field by field.
struct Point {
  x: Int
  y: String
}

/// The inner half of a key one nested field decides.
struct Inner {
  v: Int
}

/// The outer half.
struct Outer {
  i: Inner
  n: Int
}

/// A generic struct, whose **declared** short name is what a key renders as
/// and not the monomorphised layout's (#407).
struct Held<T> {
  v: T
}

/// An enum with a case that carries nothing and one that carries two parts.
enum Mark {
  Plain
  Pair(Int, Int)
}

/// A trait, so that a key can arrive in a box.
trait Named {
  fn name(self) -> String
}

/// One implementor of it.
struct Cat {
  tag: Int
}

impl Named for Cat {
  fn name(self) -> String {
    "cat"
  }
}

/// A value that nests through a reference, so a row can build a key as deep
/// as it wants. A value type may not hold itself, so the step is an `Array`.
enum Chain {
  Stop
  Link(Int, Array<Chain>)
}

/// `n` links deep, innermost first.
fn chain(n: Int) -> Chain {
  if n <= 0 {
    return Chain.Stop
  }
  return Chain.Link(n, [chain(n - 1)])
}

// --- the literals that hold no duplicate, and are not refused ---------------

/// Nothing to be a duplicate of.
test fn anEmptyLiteralIsNotADuplicate() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let emptySet: Set<Int> = Set.of()
  let emptyMap: Map<Int, Int> = Map.of()
  assertEqual(emptySet.length(), 0)?
  assertEqual(emptyMap.length(), 0)?
  Ok(())
}

/// One element is searched for among the none placed before it.
test fn oneElementIsNotADuplicate() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  assertEqual(Set.of(7).length(), 1)?
  assertEqual(Map.of(MapEntry(key: 7, value: "a")).length(), 1)?
  Ok(())
}

/// Distinct elements are all kept, in the order the collection sorts them by
/// and not the order they were written in.
test fn distinctElementsAreKeptAndSorted() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  assertEqual("{Set.of(3, 1, 2)}", "\{1, 2, 3\}")?
  assertEqual(
    "{Map.of(MapEntry(key: 2, value: 20), MapEntry(key: 1, value: 10))}",
    "\{1: 10, 2: 20\}",
  )?
  Ok(())
}

/// `1..3` and `1..<4` cover the same integers and are two different keys,
/// because `==` on a range compares the bounds a program wrote.
test fn twoRangesWrittenWithDifferentOperatorsAreTwoKeys(
) -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  assertEqual(Set.of(1..3, 1..<4).length(), 2)?
  Ok(())
}

/// One field, one level down, is enough to make two keys two keys.
test fn oneNestedFieldIsEnoughToTellTwoKeysApart() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  assertEqual(
    Set.of(Outer(i: Inner(v: 1), n: 0), Outer(i: Inner(v: 2), n: 0)).length(),
    2,
  )?
  Ok(())
}

/// The relation a duplicate is found by, observed without being refused for
/// it: `inserted` answers the receiver unchanged for an element already
/// there, so two `String`s with the same bytes make one member.
test fn anEqualElementInsertedIntoASetIsNotASecondMember(
) -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let a = "a"
  assertEqual(Set.of("ab").inserted("{a}b").length(), 1)?
  Ok(())
}

// --- the refusals -----------------------------------------------------------

/// `Set.of` was given the element `3` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aDuplicateAtTheEnd() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(1, 2, 3, 3)
  Ok(())
}

/// `Set.of` was given the element `4` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aDuplicateAtTheStart() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(4, 4, 1, 2)
  Ok(())
}

/// Two elements are each written twice: the one named is the first found
/// again in argument order, which is `4` — its second copy comes before the
/// second `7`.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `4` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn twoDuplicatesNameTheFirstFoundAgain() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(7, 4, 4, 7)
  Ok(())
}

/// A map is keyed by its keys alone, so two entries with one key are a
/// duplicate however much their values differ, and the value is not shown.
///
/// `Map.of` was given the key `1` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different key
test fn aMapNamesTheKeyAndNotTheValue() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Map.of(MapEntry(key: 1, value: "x"), MapEntry(key: 1, value: "y"))
  Ok(())
}

/// A `String` key is named by its bytes, unquoted.
///
/// `Map.of` was given the key `a` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different key
test fn aMapNamesAStringKeyAsItsBytes() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Map.of(MapEntry(key: "a", value: 1), MapEntry(key: "a", value: 2))
  Ok(())
}

/// Equal but not identical: one `String` is a literal and the other is built
/// at run time out of two pieces. They are one key.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `ab` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn stringsAreEqualByTheirBytesAndNotByIdentity() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let a = "a"
  let bad = Set.of("ab", "{a}b")
  Ok(())
}

/// The same, one level up: two structurally equal structs, one of whose
/// fields was built at run time.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `Point(x: 1, y: ab)` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn structsAreEqualFieldByField() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let a = "a"
  let bad = Set.of(Point(x: 1, y: "ab"), Point(x: 1, y: "{a}b"))
  Ok(())
}

/// Three keys differing only in a nested field, two of them the same: the
/// refusal names the nested value it walked to.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `Outer(i: Inner(v: 1), n: 0)` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aNestedFieldDecidesWhichKeyIsNamed() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(
    Outer(i: Inner(v: 1), n: 0),
    Outer(i: Inner(v: 2), n: 0),
    Outer(i: Inner(v: 1), n: 0),
  )
  Ok(())
}

/// `Set.of` was given the element `true` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aBoolElement() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(true, true)
  Ok(())
}

/// `Set.of` was given the element `()` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aUnitElement() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of((), ())
  Ok(())
}

/// A `Duration` renders in the largest of six units that divides it exactly,
/// so `3600s` is named `1h`. It is one of the families whose text no Cove
/// body writes today.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `1h` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aDurationRendersInTheUnitThatDividesIt() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(3600s, 3600s)
  Ok(())
}

/// `Set.of` was given the element `1..3` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aRangeRendersAsTheOperatorItWasWrittenWith() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(1..3, 1..3)
  Ok(())
}

/// A case that carries nothing renders as its own name alone.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `Plain` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn anEnumCaseWithoutParts() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(Mark.Plain, Mark.Plain)
  Ok(())
}

/// `Set.of` was given the element `Pair(3, 4)` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn anEnumCaseWithParts() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(Mark.Pair(3, 4), Mark.Pair(3, 4))
  Ok(())
}

/// `Set.of` was given the element `[1, 2, 3]` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn anArrayElement() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of([1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3])
  Ok(())
}

/// Two sets built from the same members in opposite orders are one value, so
/// they are one element — and the one named renders in the set's own order
/// rather than in either literal's.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `{1, 2}` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aSetOfSetsIgnoresTheOrderTheyWereWrittenIn() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(Set.of(1, 2), Set.of(2, 1))
  Ok(())
}

/// `Set.of` was given the element `{a: 1}` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aMapAsAnElement() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(
    Map.of(MapEntry(key: "a", value: 1)),
    Map.of(MapEntry(key: "a", value: 1)),
  )
  Ok(())
}

/// A builtin `Error` renders as the message it carries and not as the struct
/// it happens to be, so the refusal quotes `boom` and not
/// `Error(message: boom)`.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `boom` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn anErrorRendersAsItsMessageAlone() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(Error(message: "boom"), Error(message: "boom"))
  Ok(())
}

/// The boxed path: ADR 0064's Decision 4 admits one dynamic-layout fallback
/// per operation, and a `dyn Trait` is what reaches it. The box is looked
/// through both to find the duplicate and to name it, so what is quoted is
/// the `Cat` inside and not the trait.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `Cat(tag: 1)` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aBoxedElementIsNamedByWhatIsInTheBox() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let one: dyn Named = Cat(tag: 1)
  let other: dyn Named = Cat(tag: 1)
  let bad = Set.of(one, other)
  Ok(())
}

/// The declared short name, without the type arguments the instantiation is
/// identified by (#407).
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `Held(v: 1)` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn aGenericStructIsNamedByItsDeclaredName() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(Held(v: 1), Held(v: 1))
  Ok(())
}

/// The key is quoted between backticks and is otherwise untouched: a key
/// holding a backtick and a newline puts both in the message, and the
/// message runs onto a second line. That is the specification rather than an
/// oversight, and pinning it here is what stops a rewrite from quietly
/// "fixing" it.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `a`b
/// c` more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn nothingQuotesOrEscapesTheKeyThatIsNamed() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let odd = "a`b\nc"
  let bad = Set.of(odd, odd)
  Ok(())
}

/// Forty-two links is the deepest two keys still compare at: the backend
/// walks a key 128 steps and a link costs three. One more and the refusal is
/// the comparison's rather than this one's — see this file's header.
///
/// `Set.of` was given the element `Link(42, [Link(41, [ ... [Stop] ... ]])`
/// more than once
/// help: remove the duplicate, or give it a different element
test fn theDeepestNestingTwoKeysStillCompareAt() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(chain(42), chain(42))
  Ok(())
}

/// The one row whose refusal is not this operation's. `std.set.of` asks
/// `core.admitKey` of every element before it looks for a duplicate, so a
/// `Float` — which has no total order, because `NaN` is not equal to itself
/// — is refused by the admission and never reaches `core.refuseDuplicate`.
/// Every layout `Value.refuseDuplicate` can name is therefore one
/// `Value.admitKey` admits, and the row is here to keep that ordering fixed.
///
/// `Set.of` cannot use a `Float` as a set element
/// help: convert it to a stable key first, such as rounding to an `Int` or
/// formatting it as a `String`
test fn theAdmissionRefusesAFloatBeforeADuplicateIsLookedFor(
) -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let bad = Set.of(1.5, 1.5)
  Ok(())
}
